## Increment — comment prose rewritten under the narrow lift. `compilerOptions` values untouched.

Head `6d9c45601b` on PR #9500 (still **draft**, not enqueued, no auto-merge). Ruling taken as given: the `types` refusal is permanent, the comment staleness is mine to fix here.

### Before / after

**Before** (the stale clause, on `main`):

```jsonc
    // Measured: no test file here uses `global`, and the seven suites that use
    // `@testing-library/jest-dom` matchers `import '@testing-library/jest-dom'`
    // explicitly, so its global augmentation reaches the whole program through
    // that import (plugin-list's lesson).
```

**After** (the paragraph's conclusion — do not name `types`, and why — is untouched above this and still stands in its own words):

```jsonc
    // Re-measured here on this program's own resolved file list (objectui#8722
    // round 2) — `tsc -p tsconfig.test.json --listFiles`, never a glob, because
    // these tests sit at four different depths and any single glob reads a
    // partial population. Of its 161 test files:
    //
    //   - None uses the Node `global` object, and one could not slip in
    //     unnoticed: injecting `global.setTimeout` into a test file here is
    //     TS2304, against an unmutated exit 0. That is what keeps `types` unset
    //     safe on the Node axis, and it is the claim this paragraph rests on.
    //   - Every one of the 92 files that call `@testing-library/jest-dom`
    //     matchers now carries its own `import '@testing-library/jest-dom';`,
    //     so no file's matchers are typed only by a DIFFERENT file's import.
    //     119 files carry that import in all; the 27 beyond the 92 do not use
    //     a matcher themselves.
    //
    // Those counts are a reading taken once, not a live fact. The instrument
    // that re-derives them is `pnpm census:tsconfig-test-parity`, and the
    // column that must stay 0 for this package is `leaning`.
    //
    // What the second point does NOT claim is that any single import is
    // load-bearing. The augmentation is still PROGRAM-scoped, so removing any
    // proper subset of those 119 imports leaves the program green (measured).
    // What changed is that every matcher user is now also a supplier, so no
    // file here can be reddened by an edit to a file it does not control.
```

### One correction to the ruling's premise, and it changes a number

⚠️ The ruling says *"you measured 119 suites using matchers"*. **119 is the carrier count, not the matcher-user count.** Measured on this base, over the **161 test files in the resolved program**:

| population, in words | count |
| :-- | --: |
| test files in the tsc-resolved program | 161 |
| files that **call** jest-dom matchers | **92** |
| files that **carry** a jest-dom import | 119 |
| carriers that use no matcher themselves | 27 |

The comment uses **92** for "suites that use matchers" — the slot "seven" occupied — and states 119 separately as the carrier count, so both numbers are in the file with the population each is over named beside it. 92 is the compiler's own answer, not a regex's: ablating all 119 imports reddens exactly 92 files.

### ⭐ `global` was re-measured, not copied — and the original instrument could not have answered it

The old claim came from a grep, and **a grep cannot tell code from prose here**: six files in this program contain the English word *global* in a comment (react-i18next's global instance, `vitest.setup.base.ts` restoring "the global", a test title). None uses the Node `global` object.

So I asked the compiler instead, with a firing control: injecting `const __probe = global.setTimeout;` into a test file gives

```
packages/fields/src/widgets/ImageLightbox.test.tsx(56,17): error TS2304: Cannot find name 'global'.
```

against an unmutated **exit 0 / 0 errors**. Mutation proven on disk (marker 0 → 1, else the reading is void); restore proven by blob hash `336257388e…` equal on both sides, held by an `EXIT INT TERM` trap. ⇒ the claim holds, and now it holds because the program *enforces* it rather than because someone once grepped.

### Point 4 — only comment bytes moved

| check | reading |
| :-- | :-- |
| changed lines in the file | **28**, of which **0** are not a `//` comment line |
| parsed config, before vs after | **byte-identical** — 223 canonical bytes either side, same `compilerOptions` keys (`composite, declaration, jsx, lib, noEmit, paths`) |
| `grep -cE '"types"'` | **0** |
| firing controls, **same command** | `"noEmit"` → **1**, `"lib"` → **1** |

The controls are the point: a grep that reads 0 for `"types"` while reading 1 for two keys that *are* there is a measurement; alone it would just be a silent zero.

### Gates re-run

`tsc -p packages/fields/tsconfig.test.json` exit **0** / 0 errors · `turbo run type-check --filter @object-ui/fields` **11/11** exit 0 · `check-type-check-coverage` exit 0 (**43/43** test programs, 0 declared debt) · `census:tsconfig-test-parity` exit 0, `packages/fields` row still `161 · side-effect import · leaning 0 · node route none` · `check-published-tsconfig-tooling-exclude`, `check-control-bytes` (7667 files), `check-changeset-presence`, `check-changeset-claims`, `check:new-line-citations`, `check:comment-mask-corpus`, `check:lint-rule-coverage` — all exit 0. Control-byte self-scan over the changed file: no hits; the only non-ASCII character in it is the em dash it already used.

`gates_weakened:` **NONE.**

### Fences, re-verified on the full PR

17 files, **+48/−4**. The **only** added line in any test file across the whole PR is `import '@testing-library/jest-dom';`, fifteen times — nothing else was added to a test. No shared importer. No `plugin-timeline`, no other package's tsconfig (0 diff paths match). No test asserts the census column: the word `leaning` appears **once** in the diff, in the config comment above, never in an assertion. Still draft; I have not flipped ready, enqueued, or armed auto-merge.


